[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "00116a58-9911-57bf-b250-a7d621ba7ca6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=- \\frac{1}{10}$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5}(- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{8}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{8})+1(- \\frac{3}{10}- \\frac{6}{5})+\\sqrt{2}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})+1\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2})+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{13 \\sqrt{2}}{10}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{6}{5}- \\frac{3}{2}- \\frac{13}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{53}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=- \\frac{53}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "64afb93a-9215-5ee6-be8d-37000929e184",
