  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "30606bc8-7ba4-5845-a795-af73f32f3535",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=- \\frac{2}{3}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{3})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ - \\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3})(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}- \\sqrt{3})+(- \\frac{1}{3})(-1+\\frac{9}{2})+\\sqrt{3}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{9}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot (- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3})+(- \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}- \\frac{7}{6}- \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=- \\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5b1e7193-615b-5687-b62a-127a49c9fbc2",
