    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6cf71ed3-5ea8-56ac-892c-4eded8024adb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & 1 & \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & 1 & \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & 1 \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{5}}{4}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{4})+(-1)(\\frac{25}{12}- \\frac{15}{4})+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})\\cdot 2 \\sqrt{5}+(-1)\\cdot (- \\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "df1e74e9-418d-5922-b29b-721c4028e652",
