    "question": {
      ":question/id": "47dc3872-a925-5899-b1f5-2381598c9a3d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(A)=- \\frac{3}{4}$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & - \\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{3}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & - \\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{3}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & 2 \\sqrt{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}|+\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} & - \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2})(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{5}}{5})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(- \\frac{5}{4}+5)+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{20}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{10}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{5})+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}- \\frac{3}{2}- \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=- \\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
