  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b4cb4337-0a20-5acc-bc36-b31bbf8cb783",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sqrt{2} & 1 & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{2} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & -1 & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 \\sqrt{2} & 1 & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{2} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & -1 & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 \\sqrt{2} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}2 \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}-4)+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})(- 2 \\sqrt{2}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot (- \\frac{4}{3})+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})\\cdot 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}- \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c13d7495-0ce6-55c2-bde2-ea779e320845",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
