    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e30f7df1-d98f-5556-ab8a-32de3d386caa",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=-4$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2}{5}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} & - \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ - \\sqrt{5} & - \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{15}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{15})+\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{5}{6}- \\frac{10}{3})+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})+\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (- \\frac{5}{2})+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}-1- \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=-4\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
