  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b5abce7f-ad17-5e6d-b932-7d754b7886bd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=\\frac{14}{15}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{5})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{10})+(- \\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{2}{3}-1)+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{15}- \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{15}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})\\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{10}+(- \\frac{2}{5})\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{3})+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{14}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a81898dc-053f-57eb-98f6-81c548db5565",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
