    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0ff886fd-77a5-568e-ac62-05ead971919f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=- \\frac{7}{2}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & -1 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & -1 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ -1 & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{3})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4})+(- \\frac{2}{3})(- \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{8})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4})+(- \\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}- \\frac{1}{4}- \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=- \\frac{7}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
