  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e8a85b2e-0dbb-5c3b-9d9b-019dd8f12cc6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=- \\frac{22}{27}$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{3} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})+\\frac{1}{3}(- \\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{16}{27}+\\frac{2}{27}- \\frac{8}{27} \\\\  & =- \\frac{22}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=- \\frac{22}{27}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4abb2c10-0272-5d6b-86d5-54fe395610a3",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
